  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Katapult - Innovation and Entrepreneurship climate and blue economy</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The vision of the project is to strengthen a long-lasting ocean and climate entrepreneurship infrastructure on the African continent, furthering a joint mission of moving more people, capital and businesses towards positive impact. 

Impact &#x2013; Impact Ventures within blue economy and climate adaptation scale, attract more capital, create jobs and providing products and services in Sub-Saharan Africa.
Outcome 1 &#x2013; Dealflow generation: A pipeline of startups within the blue economy, marine litter, and climate adaptation is generated and shared with Ocean and Climate Investors and Networks

Outcome 2 &#x2013; African startups trained to a level where they are investment ready

Outcome 3 &#x2013; Investment catalyzed into program participants&#x2019; ventures (from Katapult funds and other investors)

Outcome 4 - More female founders start and scale businesses.

The project will execute two Pre-seed startups Growth Programs, tailored thematically to support entrepreneurs in the Ocean/Blue economy space through OHA and Katapult, and to support climate entrepreneurs through ATV and Katapult. To accelerate and inspire 60 African-based ocean-minded entrepreneurs and support them moving away from startup pains into the more desirable growth (i.e. scaling and landing growth investment), change the narrative about impact entrepreneurs in Africa by supporting and showcasing successful ocean-impact entrepreneurs. The African Tech Vision (ATV) team has built a highly regarded and innovative program for female tech entrepreneurs in Africa. Through this workstream, the project will scale up and professionalise the program and operations, working in lockstep with Katapult and the project partners and deliver a growth program with a focus on gender inclusion. ATV will empower and support 50 brilliant Climate founders during the 3 years of implementation while keeping a strong gender-lense aiming at 50% female founders</narrative>
  </description>
